Metal: keep a timed-out queue failed instead of reporting success

wait_for_last_submitted_command_buffer() forgets the command buffer before it
waits on it. When the wait ends in a timeout rather than a terminal command
buffer state, the member has already been cleared, so the *next* wait() finds
nothing outstanding and returns success -- a queue whose work never completed
reports healthy, and the run continues on unsynchronized data.

0020 latches failures reported by completion handlers, but a timeout is not a
completion-handler event and so was not covered.

Latch the timeout into the same async error state, from both wait sites:
wait_for_last_submitted_command_buffer() and the synchronous device-to-host
drain in submit_memcpy(). The latch is sticky, so once a wait times out every
later wait keeps reporting the failure.

Verified by forcing ACPP_METAL_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS=1 on a WarpX 2D Langmuir run:
the timeout propagates through AMReX as an async SYCL exception and aborts the
run (exit 1, 0 steps) instead of proceeding.

Note on what is deliberately NOT changed here: releasing our reference to a
still-pending command buffer on the timeout path is safe. Command buffers are
created with MTL::CommandQueue::commandBuffer(), which retains referenced
resources until completion, so the memcpy staging buffer cannot be freed while
a blit is still able to touch it.

diff --git a/src/runtime/metal/metal_queue.cpp b/src/runtime/metal/metal_queue.cpp
index d2153546..9845bb2a 100644
--- a/src/runtime/metal/metal_queue.cpp
+++ b/src/runtime/metal/metal_queue.cpp
@@ -787,6 +787,13 @@ result metal_inorder_queue::wait_for_last_submitted_command_buffer(
 
   auto completion = wait_for_metal_command_buffer(command_buffer, operation);
   command_buffer->release();
+  if (!completion.is_success()) {
+    // The command buffer has already been forgotten above, so a subsequent
+    // wait() would find nothing outstanding and report success even though this
+    // one never reached a terminal state. Latch the failure so every later wait
+    // keeps failing instead of declaring a wedged queue healthy.
+    record_metal_async_failure(_async_error_state, completion);
+  }
   return completion;
 }
 
@@ -1043,6 +1050,9 @@ result metal_inorder_queue::submit_memcpy(memcpy_operation& op, const dag_node_p
     auto completion =
         wait_for_metal_command_buffer(command_buffer, "device-to-host memcpy");
     if (!completion.is_success()) {
+      // Fail closed: the staging bytes were never published, so the queue must
+      // not look healthy to a later wait.
+      record_metal_async_failure(_async_error_state, completion);
       return completion;
     }
 
